**Proposition 10.1 (precise remaining separation).** An all-support mass floor restores uniform continuity of actual posterior normalization and therefore a finite witness cap at each fixed description budget. It does not supply a total computable cap across the complete description category. Exact semantic decoder recovery and actual acquisition of enough history to certify the supremum are different relations, even when both exact compatibility equations hold.

**Proof.** Theorem 2.3 establishes the first assertion; Theorem 9.1 establishes the second using the same original full-tail consumer. In the constructed observers, each individual fixed-history full future TV is not merely approximable but exactly rational by (5.3). For each specified finite stochastic word and strict slack, (6.5) effectively localizes a positive actual history. What is missing is an upper certificate on *all* stochastic words carried by arbitrary paid seed rejections. That word supremum survives both compatibility equations: constant early emissions prevent reweighting before the latch, and informative first-$p$ emissions have common successor laws after private-label disposal. Consequently the compatibility equations constrain future continuation without collapsing the acquisition trace semigroup.

For an individual fixed budget, a finite $H$ exists; as an individual integer it can be hard-coded if known. The theorem prohibits a single algorithm selecting suitable integers on every input budget or description. It does not assert that every individual observer has a noncomputable value or that no certified observer subclass permits effective bounds. The floor and identity-padding posterior rate quantify localization for a given word, not the search for the globally valuable word. Endpoint-law extraction or the near-optimal future-kernel rigidity of [PH] §§20–24 supplies no certificate for the earlier checker/label evolution in this high-risk family. PH45–46 effectively realizes supplied certified endpoint rows/margins; PH48 and [EF] Proposition 6.4 show that endpoint extraction can erase relevant transient histories. In this construction the obstruction is already in paid seed-rejection code words. Neither those endpoint results nor PH23.2 supplies their all-prefix upper certificate. The conclusions of [EF] Theorems 7.1/7.2 remain valid for their specified-word/same-family or relative-prior tasks, while its Open question 8.3 is answered negatively here over the entire COMPLETE class. ∎

